350003	194	2020-09-29 11:21:42	Lab Development: Lab development is partially completed. \r\na.   Table top required to carry out project experimentation of power combining of two magnetrons has been procured and installed.\r\nb.   Installation of ESD mats, earthing points and power sockets is in progress.\r\n\r\n2.   Magnetron Injection Locking Modeling and Simulation\r\na.   Preliminary injection locking numerical scheme as designed has been investigated using computational modeling. Promising results are achieved in lines with the design.\r\nb.   Various combinations of gain and bandwidth of the injection signal were tested in simulation. The trend was observed to follow the classical Adler’s Equation and theory. This analysis has furnished the requirement to develop additional feedback loop to control the characteristics of injection locking signal’s spectral bandwidth and gain, through FPGA based control circuitry. \r\nc.   Noisy excitation signal to the magnetron was also simulated to investigate the effect on magnetron jittering. Magnetron output was observed to be highly non-stationary (the frequency is varying with respect to time). As mentioned above, this analysis has furnished the requirement to develop additional feedback loops for a stabilized fully controllable power supply through Spartan Xilinx based COTs FPGA.\r\n\r\n3.   RF Devices: Indigenous design and fabrication of following high frequency RF devices are being undertaken to accomplish project experimentation in the given funds. Circulators and couplers required for the experimentation has been modeled and simulated successfully. The production drawings have also been generated. Procurement of raw material is in progress. It is pertinent to mention that limited funds (Rs. 80M) have been allocated against the approved amount (Rs. 146M). The in-house design and development of RF devices will enable completion of project in available funds. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n4.   RF Power Amplifier:\r\n\r\na.   8Wa PA. In-house design and manufacturing of Heat Sink along with packaging of amplifier has been completed. The assembly has been successfully tested on VNA and spectrum analyzer. The thermal performance of PA with the developed sink is also satisfactory.\r\n\r\nb.   100 Watt PA.\r\n1)   In-house design of 100W power amplifier has been completed. Optimization of results through different simulations is in progress.\r\n2)   The raw material for 100W amplifier has been procured. \r\n3)   RLC meter procurement for measurement of RLC components is being undertaken. This will enable optimization and fine tuning of matching circuits used in power amplifiers.\r\n\r\n6.   Control System Designing: As mentioned above, injection locking simulation studies have revealed that sophisticated control loops are required to furnish stable phase and frequency at magnetron output port. Therefore, following control loops are being studied and under development:\r\na.   Feedback loop to furnish control power supply voltage through magnetron pushing characteristics. \r\nb.   Feedback loop to furnish efficient magnetron locking bandwidth by adjusting the bandwidth and gain of the injection signal. \r\nc.   The abovementioned feedback loops will be programmed in above mentioned FPGA. The HR is being trained in the area of control implementation on board FPGAs. \r\n\r\n7.   Hardware Procurement: Project experimentation based on injection locking experimentation of a single magnetron will first be carried out to validate simulated results.\r\na.   Procurement of experimental components including coaxial circulators, coaxial terminations, waveguide transitions, waveguide adapters, power supplies and FPGA boards is in process. \r\nb.   Electronics systems required for injection locking of single magnetron including Phase Locked Loop (PLL) based ICs and Pulse Width Modulation (PWM) ICs are also being procured.\r\n\r\n8.   Anechoic Chamber. Preliminary study on in-house development of anechoic chamber is being pursued.	DSensors	Cancelled	2020-10-07 12:10:44	20	Sep 20	-20-25-20-	\N	f	\N
350002	146	2020-08-08 15:39:45	1.   Reactive Jamming. The reactive jamming software code is finalized for effective detection and tracking of fast and slow moving UAVs.\r\n\r\n2.   GPS Spoofing\r\na.   HOTAS Controlled GPS Spoofing. The counterfeit signals for 7 different directions i.e. left, right, forward, backward, up, down and center location are controlled using the Hands On Throttle And Stick (HOTAS). Using this controller, the operator can alter the GPS signal in any direction depending upon sighting of hostile UAV in real time. The alteration in GPS signal force the UAV to traverse the spoofed/ disrupted path.  \r\nb.   Internet Independent Spoofing (IIS). A procedure is formulated for implementing GPS spoofing at remote sites where internet is not available\r\n1)   Gathering of real time Ephemeris data of 10-12 satellites without internet is a challenge. \r\n2)   A capability is developed to predict the Ephemeris data for the spoofing attack accurately using GPS data, received using GPS receivers, up to 24 hours before the spoofing attack. \r\nc.   Upon receipt of 3D X-Band RADAR, target UAV tracking is possible enabling automated generation of required GPS signals capable to traverse the UAV to spoofed/ disrupted path. \r\nd.   Force Majeure has been invoked by DITD due COVID pandemic. The delivery of 3D X-Band RADAR is also delayed due to export permit processing delays caused by COVID 19 pandemic. Once 3D X-band RADAR will be received, 8-10 weeks are required for customization and testing of GPS spoofing milestone.\r\n3.   Housing of Project Eagle Hunt equipment in Marinized Container will be used as Technology demonstrator.\r\n\r\na.   The planning for housing the spoofing and jamming equipment onboard marinized container is finalized. The INTELL team along with all the received equipment will move to the facility of M/s RIMS on 17th August 2020 for the installation of equipment.\r\n\r\n1)   Direction finding antenna,  RADAR, jamming and spoofing antennas mounted on trackers will be installed on the container top. \r\n\r\n2)   HD cameras will also be mounted for 360° coverage, enabling the HOTAS operator to control the target UAV using counterfeit signals.\r\n\r\n3)   The reactive jamming and spoofing control stations will be placed inside the container.\r\n\r\n3.   Miscellaneous Studies Supporting PN Fleet and Repair Agencies undertaken during reporting period \r\n\r\na.   Analysis of S/M Besconds using Ultrasonic Testing. NRDI NDT Lab acquired MX2 Ultrasonic Testing (UT) equipment for development of training modules of Dockyard Lab staff. S/M Besconds offer passage to cables through pressure hull openings. Indigenously developed Besconds from local industry were characterized using the UT equipment for presence of air bubbles.\r\n\r\nb.   S/M Generator Armature Shaft Repair. Repair process for generator armature shaft repair of AGOSTA-70B was devised. The detailed process including machining, sleeve fitting, MIG welding of sleeve and final machining for armature shaft repair was also proposed. Presently the repair work is in progress at local industry following the INTELL team stipulated procedure.\r\n\r\nc.   S/M Generator Armature Replacement. Feasibility of replacement of main generator rotor onboard AGOSTA-70B with an armature of AGOSTA-90B was undertaken. Precautions and test practices to furnish the replacement was recommended in accordance with IEEE standards.\r\n\r\nd.   S/M Generator Door Fabrication. The fitness of alternate material along with welding process requirements, QC inspection standards, ultrasonic testing (UT) procedure in accordance with ISO Std. 17640:2018. KSEW is undertaking fabrication in lines with above mentioned dictates.	DSensors	Cancelled	2020-08-08 15:42:15	20	Jul 20	-20-25-30-25-20-	-20-25-30-25-20-	f	\N
