350014	686	2022-04-27 03:34:03	\r\n1.   Development of Scaled Down Model of GSP. A scaled down model of GSP has been developed in-house to validate the calculated range of motion and forces for the platform and linkages. The scaled down model has been designed to retain the full range of motion in roll (± 25?) and pitch (± 15?) as outlined in the URD, along with a capability to carry upto 1kg of payload. Electronic components for scaled down model have been procured along with raw materials for structure and frame and construction has been completed. The range of motion in roll and pitch for the system, acquired using kinematics models have been validated using the scaled down model. Development of scaled down model will provide confidence in full scale GSP development. Point recommended to be dropped.\r\n\r\n2.   Algorithm Development. The control algorithm for GSP has been extensively studied and different approaches have been implemented in Matlab. The most optimized approach in terms of system response time was observed using the inverse kinematics model along with PID. Fine tuning of parameters for PID has been carried out. Additionally, an optimized, lightweight version of algorithm to run it on industrial mini pc in the field has been developed. \r\n\r\n3.   PN Ship Visit For Finalization of Installation Option/Mechanism On-board PN Ship. Upon directions from NHQ to select feasible options shared by NRDI INTELL for installation of Ship based Line of Sight (LoS) Video Telemetry System (VTS) with Gyro Stabilized Platform (GSP), GM NCW recommended Option-1 (Portable platform) with Radome for relaying of LoS VTS vide letter no PN/PN DOCKYARD/W AND S WING/ GM (C4I)/03/12818126 dated 21 Mar 22. INTELL is pursuing the development in lines with the proposed approach. \r\n\r\n4.   Assembly of Full-Scale Model. Design of full-scale model was completed, and QC was carried out on the design. Parts were manufactured according to design and assembly has been carried out. QC of assembled parts has also been carried out. Integration of electronics in full scale model is completed. Developed algorithms have also been used to verify movement and stabilization capabilities of full scale GSP model. Integration of MOTUS IMU has been carried out in full scale model. Load testing of full scale model with antenna tracker has been carried out and performance of the platform was found satisfactory within the full operating range of the platform.\r\n\r\n5.   Development of Test Bench. A test bench is being developed to verify the range of motion of full-scale model of GSP. Updated parameters for this design have been used in forward kinematics equations to verify the range of motion. CAD design of the model has been developed based on the calculated values. QC of design and simulation of stress and forces have also been carried out. Manufacturing of metal linkages and parts is underway. Additionally, control algorithm of test bench which includes multiple testing protocols and extensive reporting on performance of GSP platforms, is also under development. A GUI for ease of operator usage is also under development for GSP test bench. A study was carried out to reduce unwanted wobbling in the platform when it is operated under load. The cause of this additional movement was determined to be the inherent freedom of movement that is available in ball joints that are used to connect linkages to the platform. Some spring dampers were used to mitigate the additional movement and the issue was resolved, thereby improving platform performance.\r\n\r\n6.   Procurement. Electronics components for the scaled-down model have been procured along with raw materials for structure and frame. Shortlisted stepper motors and drivers for full-scale models as well as the universal joints have also been procured. Mechanical and electrical components for test bench are being considered and procurement will be initiated once parts have been selected. Motor selection for high torque stepper motors for test bench has been completed and quotations have been obtained from vendors. Items are raised in minute and order will be placed as soon as approval is received.	DSensors	Finalized	2022-05-10 03:01:00	25	Apr 22	-19-25-	\N	f	\N
350004	689	2022-04-27 04:02:23	\r\n1.   Change in The Scope of Project. The project scope has been revised in VCNS IHD meeting, vide reference PN/ NDR DIV/ R AND D DTE/03/3240447 dated 05 Oct 21. Operating range of UCAV is limited to 200 km and BLOS operation requirements are eliminated. In this regard, SATCOM and EOIR systems are also removed from the scope. The timeline is also revised to 18 months. Following documents have been submitted in lines with the requirement of IHD meeting:\r\n\r\na.   Revised timeline and itemized cost were forwarded vide letter PN/DG NRDI/ R AND D Wing/03/11125889 dated 02 Nov 21.\r\n\r\nb.   Itemized cost estimates for procurement plan and pertinent details related to procurement source was forwarded vide reference PN/DG NRDI/ At PNS JAUHAR/03/11526644 dated 29 Nov 21. \r\n\r\nc.   Combined draft work order amounting to PKR 238M and corresponding technical and budgetary proposal were forwarded vide letter PN/DG NRDI/At JAUHAR/03/11715770 dated 10 Dec 21.\r\n\r\n2.   Phase-II Work Order. Work order for phase 2 is still awaited. Case for CFA approval is under progress at NHQ. Dir R&D requested to forward list of hardware for hardware milestones and technical proposal for further processing of case, vide reference PN/NRD DIV/R AND D DTE/03/3563370 dated 1 Mar 22. Letter add\r\n\r\n3.   Critical Design of UCAV. Study on selection of actuators for control surfaces and landing gear underway. Electrohydraulic servo valve systems are being considered to implement a fully redundant control system for control surfaces. Upon conclusion of study, quotations will be requested from vendors for relevant components/systems. Design for integration of fuel circuit and engine into airframe is underway. Design of linkage for retraction mechanism of landing gear is also in progress. Actuators for control surfaces and landing gear will be selected after design studies are completed. \r\n\r\n4.   Test Bench for ROTAX Engine. Construction of test bench along with integration of mechanical and electrical components has been completed. Custom designed instrument panel for monitoring engine parameters during testing has also been completed. A gascolator which is required for the fuel circuit in order to filter the fuel before reaching the engine has also been procured. Design of Bowden cable and throttle control mechanism has also been completed and manufacturing of components will be initialized in Jan ’22. Final QC and safety checks will be carried out once manufactured parts will be received. \r\n\r\n5.   Machine Shop. Hartford CNC has been received at serial production facility and placed in the designated area.  Commissioning of the CNC is in progress and will be completed in early Feb ’22. Requirements for air compressor have been finalized. An external enclosed area will be designated outside machine shop for the compressor. On-site survey of the air compressor enclosure has been carried out and construction work has been started. A curing oven for the manufacturing of large parts has been designed in-house. Design specifications have been forwarded to vendor and item has been designed. Initial QC will be carried out once oven is ready.\r\n\r\n6.   Team development. In-house interviews for hardware design engineers have been conducted and suitable candidates for hiring have been shortlisted.	DSensors	Finalized	2022-05-10 03:01:57	25	Apr 22	-19-25-	\N	f	\N
350015	708	2022-05-11 23:05:38	1.   Following activities pertaining to in-house development and experimentation were carried out:\r\n\r\na.   Small Scale Weapon Field Testing. Preliminary Field testing was of 2.5 KW setup was conducted at RDS NATHIAGALI vide reference PN/NRDI/R AND D WING/03/12427313 dated 16 Feb 22. The interference was observed with drone as target. Multiple targets such as Low Noise Amplifiers (LNAs), Phase Frequency Detectors (PFDs), RF rectifiers and RF Power Meters are being evaluated for disruptive demonstration. Field testing is scheduled to be conducted in early May 2022.\r\n\r\nb.   Horn Antenna Improvement. Horn antenna has been redesigned and fabricated resulting in better efficiency.  A testing was carried out at SPF with one horn antenna used for transmission, and another used for reception. A 47 dBm signal was transmitted at 2.45 GHz, and 13 dBm was received at reception antenna at 10 m, resulting in propagation loss of 34 dB. The exercise was aimed to simulate the in-band damage effect on a missile seeker. The 13 dBm signal is disruptive for LNAs and PFDs used in RADAR systems of missile seekers. Point recommended to be dropped.\r\n\r\nc.   X-Band Experiment. RF devices are being designed and fabricated at X-band for use in power combination of pulsed M1475A 25 kW magnetrons. Power supplies and pulse modulators were procured and have undergone preliminary testing to ensure desired voltages are achieved at pertinent test points. The modulators will now be tested with X-band magnetrons.\r\n\r\n2.   RF Power Amplifier. Biasing circuit PCB has been redesigned with adequate track width suitable for high drain current. The Matching networks of the 100 Watt amplifier have been redesigned employing Simplified Real Frequency Technique (SRFT) for output MN and Bounded Performance Technique (BPT) for input MN. PCB fabrication has been completed; PCB component population is in progress and will available for testing in May 22.\r\n\r\n \r\n3.   Following studies are being conducted to formulate research plans for combination of X-band magnetrons for more effective DEW design: \r\n\r\na.   A pulsed power supply based on Pulsed Forming Networks (PFN) required to drive pulsed magnetrons is currently being designed in-house.\r\n\r\nb.   Preliminary design of RF devices including circulators and directional couplers at X-band (9 GHz) has been finalized. RF devices are under fabrication and testing stages.\r\n\r\n4.   Technical proposal amounting PKR 232M for Phase II pertaining to power combination of multiple J-band pulsed magnetrons of redundant AM-39 has been submitted to NHQ for approval. An anechoic RF chamber will also be designed and fabricated to enable shielded high-power testing of the magnetron combination experiment with adequate safety measures. Point recommended to be dropped.\r\n\r\n5.   The proposal for future plan was also submitted vide Ref no PN/DGNRDI/RNDWING/03/11409776 dated 21ST Nov 21 in which Power Combination of multiple 25Kw pulsed J-band magnetrons, to achieve 150 kW combined power, is to be undertaken. Following are the salient highlights of the approach. Point recommended to be dropped.\r\n\r\na.   Pulsed operation philosophy will yield lower operational cost, lesser cooling requirement, higher lethality compared to CW.\r\n\r\nb.   J-band RF devices will be smaller in size and can be efficiently tailored for installation onboard naval platform.\r\n\r\n6.   Scan Eagle. INTELL NRDI RF team visited PNS MAKRAN to conduct defect diagnosis in the RF end of Scan Eagle GCS AIM and Directional Antenna. \r\n\r\na.   Antenna was found to be mechanically deformed and mismatched at S-band.    Defect rectification was attempted on-site and antenna VSWR parameters were significantly improved. INTELL was also approached to design a new antenna based on solid dish, for which the team took all design measurements on-site. The design study for the antenna is currently in progress. \r\n\r\nb.   RF paths of three of the AIMs were also tested at INTELL on VNA. The mechanical filters of two of the systems were found degraded.	DSensors	Finalized	2022-06-04 04:42:50	25	Apr 22	-19-25-	\N	f	\N
350010	710	2022-05-11 23:11:26	1.   Harbor Acceptance Trials. Harbor Acceptance Trials of all 19 VFDs has been carried out and cleared by DY team. Point recommended to be dropped.\r\n \r\n2.   Correspondence. Following correspondence has been carried out. \r\n\r\na.   NRDI INTELL team has critically analyzed the phenomena of insulation drop onboard PNS KHALID. The insulation drop occurs while connecting MO with NRDI develop VFDs. The deployment of insulation barrier in between them has rectified the insulation issue and improved the protection of VFD from external surges/ground faults.  Point recommended to be dropped.\r\nb.   4 to 20 mA signal provision design also revised. Opto-coupler based solid state relays coupled with insulation barrier are used for higher insulation and better protection of VFDs. Point recommended to be dropped.\r\nc.   DY team has requested covers be made for DIN Rail connectors on VFD control panel for DK1 to protect vapor ingress vide reference PN/ PN DOCKYARD/ S/M WING/ GM (ME S/M WING)/ 03/13019033 dated 25 Apr 22. Same have been designed and manufactured in-house for DK1 and additionally for FC1, FC2, and DK2. Subsequently, the manufactured covers will be handed over to DY team in due course for installation onboard S/M.\r\n\r\n3.   Documentation Finalization Meeting. Comments regarding provided documentation were received from DY team vide reference PN/ PN DOCKYARD/ S/M WING/ GM (ME S/M WING)/ 03/12844970 dated 4 Apr 22. All comments of the second review have been addressed by NRDI INTELL team. The updated documentation has been shared with DY team vide reference PN/NRDI/ R&D WING/ 03/ 13096133 dated 21 Apr 22. It is further highlighted in the response that the PCB design layouts/drawings are IP of OEM. Moreover, adequate spares of VFD are already available in stock. Therefore, PCB repair is adjudged as Level-5 maintenance activity, to be undertaken by NRDI INTELL, and the said drawings may not be shared. \r\n\r\n4.   Patternization of VFDs. Detailed part list for all 10 applications of NRDI developed VFDs have been forwarded to NCMA vide reference PN/NRDI/R AND D WING/03/12841118 dated 24 Mar 22 for initialization of patternization process. Comments regarding shared patternization list were received by NRDI team. All comments were addressed, and updated patternization list has been shared with NCMA vide reference PN/ HQ NRDI/ R&D WING/03/13119482 dated 22 Apr 22.\r\n\r\n5.   Acceptance of Project Completion. All 19 VFD have been installed onboard PN S/M. Moreover, multiple sea trials have been conducted successfully. In view of that, MTSS has forwarded Milestone completion certificate against Milestone M10 to acceptance committee for endorsement vide letter MTSS/20/112 dated 21 Apr 22.	DSensors	Finalized	2022-06-04 04:43:28	25	Apr 22	-19-25-	\N	f	\N
